 This paper presented GBCA for original-node, untargeted poisoning attacks under separate structural and attribute constraints. Its coordination mechanism executes a selected edge modification and then runs AE-PR on the modified graph, using the two endpoints as restart seeds to locate important nodes for attribute attack. In the matched Citeseer comparison with a GCN victim, serial coordination lowered attacked accuracy by 4.20 and 4.60 percentage points relative to parallel combination at the 5\% and 10\% structural budgets. Across nine search settings on Cora, Citeseer, and Pubmed, Granular-Ball search yielded larger accuracy drops than matched K-Means while reducing search runtime by 9.3\%--29.5\% and peak memory by 8.9\%--28.0\%. Direct node-level search achieved larger accuracy drops in eight of these nine settings, at higher time and memory costs. These results support endpoint-conditioned coordination and quantify the effectiveness--cost trade-off of hierarchical structural search within the evaluated graph and budget range.
 
+The separate five-seed Citeseer follow-up, using a degree global source, pre-flip diffusion, and frozen structural features, gives the lowest mean accuracy when both stages use attribute weights. However, paired tests do not establish a reliable gain or interaction, and the incomplete matched selector comparison does not establish AE-PR superiority over ordinary PR, Degree, or Random. These diagnostics delimit the current evidence for attribute weighting; they do not establish the necessity of post-flip diffusion.
+
 The findings are limited to transductive node classification on the evaluated graphs and victim architectures. Greedy hierarchical refinement does not guarantee globally optimal structural actions, and surrogate guidance depends on the quality of fixed pseudo labels. The LLM module depends on generation quality and a meaningful correspondence between the generated vocabulary and model features. Lexical and feature-similarity constraints provide update-level controls intended to promote stealthiness; they do not establish text-level semantic equivalence, preservation of graph distributions, or resistance to detection. The reported search costs exclude LLM generation and feature projection, so the measured savings do not quantify end-to-end attack cost.
 
