+\section{Controlled Follow-up Protocol and Accounting}
+\label{app:followup_details}
+The follow-ups use the processed Citeseer graph with 2,110 nodes, 183/366 edge flips at the 5\%/10\% budgets, and a retrained GCN victim (16 hidden units, dropout 0.5). The effective split is DeepRobust \texttt{nettack}: the Citeseer loader fixes this setting even though the launcher records \texttt{split\_data=normal}. The evaluation set contains 1,899 validation-plus-test nodes. Seeds 15--19 define paired attack and victim replicates; structural, selector, generation, and victim random streams are separated. Original inputs, clean predictions, saved attacked predictions, and structural-action replay are checked before a block enters a summary. Seed is the statistical unit; nodes are not treated as independent replicates.
+
+Global guidance selects a ratio of 0.10. The factorial protocol resolves exact global-score ties by ascending node ID; its 10,980 recorded rounds have 1,840--2,011 distinct scores among 2,110 nodes, with positive score ranges throughout. Thus these degree-source runs avoid the all-tied uniform-source configuration, although this diagnostic does not prove ranking quality. Local score ties also use node ID. The selector suite retains the earlier global NumPy sorting rule and is analyzed separately. Ordinary PR is computed by \texttt{networkx.pagerank} with damping 0.72, tolerance $10^{-12}$, and at most 1,000 iterations; convergence is required.
+
+Both suites admit up to a 0.12 fraction of nodes per attribute round, with no additional distinct-node cap and at most ten visits per node. Other shared settings are an adaptation-word limit of 80, a maximum of 70 newly activated vocabulary dimensions per update, a current-vector cosine threshold of 0.35, and group sizes of 2--4. Generation uses \texttt{llama3.2:1b-instruct-fp16}, temperature zero, and a seed derived from the run seed. These runs use the recorded fallback vocabulary (\texttt{feature\_i} placeholders), so they are feature-space diagnostics and do not establish natural-text semantic preservation. The structural model always uses original features; the local selector uses its arm's evolving attributes. This design leaves the global-to-structure effect active but does not measure feedback from modified attributes into later structural search.
+
+Every included run records 2,110 changed nodes and 21,100 node visits, exhausting ten visits to every node. Consequently, the selector differences concern the sequence and trajectory of updates, not a smaller final set of modified nodes. Table~\ref{tab:followup_consumption} reports recorded net feature-entry changes, which differ across arms despite shared limits. Factorial entry counts are identical across the two budgets within each seed and arm. The retained artifacts contain graphs, predictions, and change counts but no final feature matrix or feature-norm measurements; final perturbation amplitude cannot be independently recovered. The local cosine threshold is not a guarantee about the final distance from clean features.
+
+The factorial matrix contains all five complete four-arm seed blocks at both budgets. For selectors, the 5\% runs for seeds 17--19 are incomplete or have inconsistent identity records, and the 10\% Random run at seed 19 failed. Only complete blocks enter Table~\ref{tab:local_candidate_strategy_sensitivity}; available arms of an incomplete block are excluded. Missing runs are not imputed, and preliminary selector subsets are not pooled with the factorial cohort. Paired $t$ intervals and sign-flip tests are exploratory at these small sample sizes; with five seeds the minimum attainable exact two-sided sign-flip $p$ is 0.0625.
